<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden Horde</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golden Horde was the Mongol khanate in the western steppe, founded by descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s son Jochi. It ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a> and held the Rus' principalities in tributary subjection from the thirteenth century, forming one of the successor states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-horde.jpg" alt="Golden Horde" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vorziblix · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Horde_flag_1339.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muscovy kept paying the Crimean khans regular ‘gifts’, in effect tribute, until the Treaty of Constantinople in 1700, long after the <b>Golden Horde</b> had vanished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time it expanded eastwards: Algirdas defeated the <b>Golden Horde</b> at the Battle of Blue Waters around 1362, opening the way to Kiev and Podolia, and marched on Moscow three times between 1368 and 1372.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Yury married a sister of Uzbek Khan of the <b>Golden Horde</b> and obtained the khan's patent (yarlyk) for the grand principality of Vladimir in 1317, beginning a bitter rivalry with the princes of Tver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kazakh Khanate grew out of the political world of the White Horde, the eastern wing of the <b>Golden Horde</b>, whose khans descended from Jochi, the eldest son of Genghis Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of the principalities became tributaries of the <b>Golden Horde</b>, and the lands of Rus' were later divided between Lithuania, Poland and the rising principality of Moscow.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Diplomatically, Mamluk relations with the <b>Golden Horde</b>, the Ilkhanate, the Ottomans and European powers such as Venice and Aragon confirmed its role as a critical player in regional politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
